# Cloudflare-origin allowlist (Layer 2 of WS1 defense-in-depth).
#
# Only the Cloudflare edge may reach the public origin. `remote_ip` matches the
# DIRECT TCP PEER — which, for a CF-proxied (orange-cloud) hostname, is always a
# Cloudflare edge IP. This is UNAFFECTED by `trusted_proxies cloudflare` (that
# only rewrites `client_ip` to the real end-user). The prior implementation used
# `client_ip` + a named source `cloudflare` — invalid syntax that crash-looped
# Caddy at boot (PR #63), and wrong semantics even with literal CIDRs.
#
# {$CLOUDFLARE_CIDRS} is a space-separated list rendered into .env by
# `maintain.py cloudflare-cidrs` (fail-closed; weekly cron). {$EXTRA_ALLOWED_IP}
# is an operator escape hatch (a trusted admin IP/CIDR) — leave blank to disable.
#
# `abort` silently drops the connection (no response) so we never leak that a
# host exists behind a non-CF probe. Tailnet site blocks do NOT import this.
(cloudflare-allowlist) {
    @not_cf not remote_ip {$CLOUDFLARE_CIDRS} {$EXTRA_ALLOWED_IP}
    abort @not_cf
}
